  <h1 class="page-title">Write It for the Future You</h1>

  <p class="intro">
    Operational ticket notes should function like packet captures for human events:
    what happened, when it happened, who participated, what changed, and what happened next.
  </p>

  <article class="card article">
    <h2>The lesson</h2>

    <p>
      During after-hours incidents, document what actually happened —
      not what you later assume happened.
    </p>

    <p>
      A missed call is not a conversation.<br />
      A voicemail is not confirmation.<br />
      A message sent is not acknowledgement.<br />
      A conversation on the 11th is not a conversation on the 8th.
    </p>

    <h2>Capture these explicitly</h2>

    <ul>
      <li>Timestamp</li>
      <li>Contact attempt</li>
      <li>Contact method</li>
      <li>Whether contact was actually made</li>
      <li>What was requested</li>
      <li>What action was taken</li>
      <li>Outcome</li>
      <li>Next owner / next action</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Example</h2>

    <p><strong>Bad:</strong></p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>Contacted Andrew.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p><strong>Better:</strong></p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>09/08 03:14 — Called Andrew; no answer. Voicemail left requesting callback.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <p>Then later:</p>

    <blockquote>
      <p>09/11 10:22 — Spoke with Andrew. Reviewed incident and confirmed follow-up action.</p>
    </blockquote>

    <h2>Why this matters</h2>

    <p>
      Weeks later, the ticket should tell the story without requiring reconstruction
      from Teams, Zoom, phone history, email, or memory.
    </p>

    <p>
      Treat operational notes like a packet capture:
      <strong>what happened, when it happened, who participated, what changed, and what happened next.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      Future Daph should be able to replay the incident from the notes.
    </p>
  </article>

